# Observability and alerting

## Log events

`log()` writes one JSON line to stdout: no metrics client, no tracer, and
package.json has no runtime dependencies on purpose. **Every metric here is
derived from those lines** — a new metric is a field on an existing event.

| event | level | emitted by | read by |
|---|---|---|---|
| `boot` | info | `src/index.ts` | deploy marker on every dashboard |
| `ingest.batch` | info | `workers/ingest.ts` | freshness + volume |
| `rollup.done` | info | `workers/rollup.ts` | rollup lateness |
| `unhandled` | error | `problem()` in `api/middleware.ts` | the only paging error signal |

- Names are `noun.verb` and **never parameterised** — `ingest.batch`, not
  `ingest.batch.site-123`; the collector indexes on `event` and bills
  cardinality. `boot` predates the rule; add no second exception. No `ts`
  field either: arrival is stamped upstream — never order two events from a tick.
- `warn` and `debug` are in the `Level` union and unused deliberately. Reaching
  for `warn` almost always means you want a field on an existing `info` event.
- Errors are half-blind. `problem()` logs nothing for a `DomainError`, so a
  provider posting garbage looks exactly like one posting nothing — alert on
  `count`. And `unhandled` carries only `message`, because `problem(e)` never
  receives the `Req`: the one event that pages names no route and no `siteId`.
- `rollup.done` logs raw `wh`. Logs are not a presentation boundary, so ADR 001
  does not apply; dashboards divide by 1000 at render time.

## Redaction, and what still leaks

`REDACT` in `lib/logger.ts` matches top-level keys by exact name. Three holes:

- It is shallow. `log("info", "x", { raw: { mpan } })` publishes the MPAN. Never
  log a whole request body or `RawReading`; name the fields you want.
- `siteId` *is* an MPAN (`validateSiteId` enforces 13 digits) and is not in the
  list. Treat it as personal data: it earns its place in `rollup.done` because
  ops cannot triage without it. A new event carrying it needs a privacy sign-off.
- Keys are filtered, values never are, and `unhandled` ships a whole stringified
  error as `message`. `NotFound` already builds `site ${siteId} not found`; the
  day such a string escapes as a non-`DomainError`, the MPAN is published.

## Metrics, all log-derived

| metric | derivation | note |
|---|---|---|
| `ingest_readings_total` | sum of `ingest.batch.count` | **attempted, not persisted** |
| `ingest_last_seen` | age of newest `ingest.batch` | the freshness SLI |
| `rollup_lateness` | now − newest `rollup.done` | per `siteId`, p99 |
| `unhandled_total` | count of `unhandled` | paging |

`count` is `insertReadings`' `rows.length` — rows handed to the DB, not a write
confirmation, and its statement holds three placeholders against `rows.flatMap`
params (3 × N), so a multi-row batch cannot land as written. Reconcile nightly.

Two blind spots share the ingest path. `ingestBatch` validates inside a `.map()`
with no try/catch, so one bad row throws before `insertReadings` and **no
`ingest.batch` line is written at all** — a poison reading reads as a stall, not
a volume drop. `POST /v1/readings` inserts directly and logs nothing: unmetered.

## SLOs

- Ingest freshness: a batch lands within **15 min**, 99.5% of half-hours.
- Rollup lateness: p99 under **3 × `ROLLUP_INTERVAL_MS`** (5 min by default, so
  15 min). Three intervals deliberately — one skipped tick is normal.
- No scheduler lives here: `ingestBatch` and `rollupSite` are exported uncalled
  and `rollupIntervalMs` is parsed with no reader; both ticks are the caller's.
- Billing API 99.9% monthly, measured at the edge: `unhandled` misses hard crashes.

## Alerts

| alert | condition | pages? |
|---|---|---|
| `ingest-stalled` | no `ingest.batch` for 30 min | yes, 24/7 |
| `ingest-volume-drop` | count < 40% of same half-hour last week | yes, business hours |
| `unhandled-spike` | > 5 in 5 min | yes |
| `rollup-late` | lateness > 30 min | no, ticket |
| `rollup-span-wide` | `rollup.done.days` > 40 | no, ticket |

The billing path has no `log` call of its own: a `no_tariff` 422 or a wrong
`pence` is silent, and only an unexpected throw surfaces, as `unhandled`.

## Incidents worth remembering

- **Kingfisher** (Nov 2024). A provider raised payload size to ~2,000 readings;
  `raw.slice(0, cfg.ingestBatchSize)` dropped the rest while `ingest.batch` still
  reported `count: 500`. Eleven days lost. **Never alert on a capped number.**
- **Redact-5** (Feb 2025). MPANs reached the log store for fourteen months as
  `rollup.done.siteId` while `mpan` sat in `REDACT`; retention there is now 30
  days. Name-keyed redaction loses to a rename.
- **Quiet Quarter** (Jun 2025). Tariff precedence (ADR 002) was applied wrongly
  and a cohort was undercharged for a quarter; a customer found it, not us.
  Lesson: **a branch choosing between candidates must log which one it chose.**
  Still open: `chargeFor` takes `active[0]`, the case ADR 002 calls wrong, and
  logs nothing — the same quarter is reproducible today.
